# Colgate’s AI ‘customers’ reach 90% of human test-retest reliability, @HowToPrompt__ claims

@HowToPrompt__ describes a Colgate method that turns AI customers’ written product reactions into numerical survey scores.
...
According to @HowToPrompt__, a Colgate paper tested synthetic consumers against 57 personal-care surveys and 9,300 human responses. The post says Semantic Similarity Rating converts written product reactions into numerical scores, reaching 90% of human test-retest reliability. That’s a comparison with how consistently humans answer repeat surveys—not the “90% accurate” survey data @politicalmath described.
...
@HowToPrompt__A toothpaste company has quietly killed the entire market research industry and nobody is talking about it. Colgate published a paper showing you can predict real purchase intent by simply asking LLMs to roleplay customers. And this is beyond insane. If you ask an AI, "Rate this product from 1 to 5," it gives safe, middle-of-the-road garbage. So researchers invented a method called Semantic Similarity Rating (SSR). Instead of asking the AI for a number, they asked it to roleplay. They gave the LLM a demographic profile. They showed it a product concept. And they asked it to write down its raw, unfiltered thoughts. Then, they used a semantic model to translate those written thoughts into a numerical score. The results are staggering. Tested against 57 real personal care product surveys and 9,300 human responses, the synthetic consumers hit 90% of human test-retest reliability. They perfectly mirrored how different age brackets and income levels react to price changes. And they provided detailed, qualitative feedback that was deeper and more critical than what actual humans wrote. This destroys the economics of traditional market research. You don't need to wait a month to see if a product will sell. You can simulate 1,000 hyper-targeted customer interviews overnight. You can A/B test pricing across every demographic instantly.@Nze
